{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YFppTDFvxQDanbPmdm7SgC","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Tasks: Audit: android homescreen widgets\n2\t\n3\tid: opinion\n4\tanalyst: @analyst\n5\tdate: 2026-10-15\n6\t\n7\t## Scope\n8\t\n9\tThis is a **report-only** job. The deliverable is one markdown file,\n10\t`docs/reports/android-homescreen-widgets.md`. It answers two questions: can\n11\tsolyto ship Android home-screen widgets, and how would we build them?\n12\t**No code, Gradle, manifest, `patch/`, `frontend/` or `fdroid/` changes.** If\n13\ta prototype ever seems necessary to answer a question, stop and record it in\n14\tthe report as an open question. Do not build one.\n15\t\n16\t`docs/reports/` does not exist yet. Creating it (by writing the report file)\n17\tis expected.\n18\t\n19\t## Context gathered by the analyst (for the developer)\n20\t\n21\t- The native side is small. `android/app/src/main/java/app/solyto/` holds\n22\t  `MainActivity` (extends `BridgeActivity`), `PullToRefreshLayout`, and one\n23\t  custom plugin, `PullToRefreshPlugin`, registered with\n24\t  `registerPlugin(...)`. That plugin is the existing pattern for a custom\n25\t  JS↔native bridge.\n26\t- `AndroidManifest.xml` has one activity (`singleTask`). Its intent filters\n27\t  are MAIN/LAUNCHER, SEND text/plain (→ `https://my.solyto.app/share?q=`),\n28\t  and VIEW on `@string/custom_url_scheme`. It has no receivers or services,\n29\t  and its only permission is INTERNET.\n30\t- Auth: the bearer token, its expiry, and the user live in **WebView\n31\t  localStorage** under the key `auth` (`frontend/src/lib/state/Auth.svelte.ts`,\n32\t  `LS_AUTH_KEY`). The token expires (`expires_at`) and is renewed through\n33\t  `apiRoutes.auth.refresh`. Native code cannot read WebView localStorage\n34\t  directly. This is the main architectural problem for any widget that shows\n35\t  live data.\n36\t- The API base URL is also in localStorage (`custom_api_url`, in\n37\t  `frontend/src/lib/config/platform.ts`). Its default is `https://api.solyto.app`.\n38\t- `frontend/` is vendored. Any JS-side bridge call (for example \"push widget\n39\t  data to native\") would have to land in the canonical `../app` repo, gated\n40\t  on `IS_MOBILE`. The report must say this explicitly.\n41\t- Distribution: GitHub Releases plus F-Droid (`fdroid/app.solyto.yml`,\n42\t  `scripts/build-fdroid.sh`, offline, Node 20). New native dependencies must\n43\t  be FOSS and available from Maven Central or Google's Maven repository, with\n44\t  no proprietary or GMS dependencies.\n45\t- SDK levels: minSdk 24, target and compile 36, Java only (no Kotlin set up\n46\t  yet; check `android/build.gradle` and `android/app/build.gradle` to confirm).\n47\t\n48\t## Task breakdown\n49\t\n50\tTASK-1: Inventory the current Android/Capacitor setup that is relevant to widgets: Java vs. Kotlin, AGP/Gradle versions, AndroidX deps, Capacitor plugins in use, minSdk/targetSdk, and existing intent handling. Summarize it as a \"current state\" section.\n51\t  files (read-only): android/build.gradle, android/app/build.gradle, android/variables.gradle, android/app/capacitor.build.gradle, android/capacitor.settings.gradle, android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml, android/app/src/main/java/app/solyto/*.java, capacitor.config.json, package.json\n52\t  depends: none\n53\t  risk: low. It is a read-only inventory.\n54\t\n55\tTASK-2: Research and compare the technical options for widgets in a Capacitor app: (a) classic `AppWidgetProvider` + `RemoteViews` (Java, no new deps); (b) Jetpack Glance (needs Kotlin + Compose compiler); (c) existing community Capacitor widget plugins, checked for maintenance status, Capacitor 7 compatibility, licence, and F-Droid suitability. Cover API-level constraints such as collection widgets/`RemoteCollectionItems` and API 31+ widget features versus minSdk 24.\n56\t  files: docs/reports/android-homescreen-widgets.md (new)\n57\t  depends: TASK-1\n58\t  risk: medium. External library status changes over time, so cite sources with dates and do not assert anything from memory.\n59\t\n60\tTASK-3: Analyse the data/auth problem: how a widget gets data and credentials when the token lives in WebView localStorage. Compare at least: (1) the web app pushes a pre-rendered widget snapshot to native via a custom Capacitor plugin (SharedPreferences), so there are no native network calls; (2) the web app hands the token and API URL to native storage, and the widget fetches through WorkManager or `updatePeriodMillis`, with token-expiry/refresh handling; (3) static \"shortcut\" widgets with no data at all. Note the security implications of copying the token out of the WebView (for example EncryptedSharedPreferences, now deprecated, versus Keystore).\n61\t  files: docs/reports/android-homescreen-widgets.md\n62\t  depends: TASK-1\n63\t  risk: medium. This is the decisive section, and it touches auth design. Present it as options, not a decision.\n64\t\n65\tTASK-4: Map candidate widgets to existing features and API endpoints (for example today's todos, next calendar events, quick-add/check-in shortcut, pinned note), using `frontend/src/lib/config/apiRoutes.ts` and the state classes. For each candidate, give the data it needs, whether it needs interaction (tap-to-complete versus open-in-app deep link), and a rough complexity rating.\n66\t  files (read-only): frontend/src/lib/config/apiRoutes.ts, frontend/src/lib/state/Todos.svelte.ts, frontend/src/lib/state/Calendars.svelte.ts, frontend/src/lib/state/CheckInData.svelte.ts; output in docs/reports/android-homescreen-widgets.md\n67\t  depends: TASK-3\n68\t  risk: low. It is descriptive, but the final widget selection belongs to the maintainer, so it should be presented as candidates.\n69\t\n70\tTASK-5: Describe how widget taps would open the app at a specific route (PendingIntent → `MainActivity` → `loadUrl(\"https://my.solyto.app/<route>\")`, the same approach as `handleShareIntent`), including the `singleTask`/`onNewIntent` behaviour.\n71\t  files (read-only): android/app/src/main/java/app/solyto/MainActivity.java, android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml; output in report\n72\t  depends: TASK-1\n73\t  risk: low. The pattern already exists in the code.\n74\t\n75\tTASK-6: Assess cross-cutting impact and constraints: vendored-frontend workflow (JS changes go to `../app`, gated on `IS_MOBILE`), F-Droid build implications (new Gradle deps, Kotlin plugin, reproducibility, `fdroid/app.solyto.yml` flagged), i18n (widget strings in Android `res/values-*` for en/de/fr/es versus the web i18n tables), light/dark theming with the existing `colors.xml`, battery and update-frequency limits, and testing approach.\n76\t  files (read-only): scripts/build-fdroid.sh, scripts/vendor.sh, fdroid/app.solyto.yml, .github/workflows/release.yml, android/app/src/main/res/values/colors.xml; output in report\n77\t  depends: TASK-2, TASK-3\n78\t  risk: low. It is analysis only, but any recommendation that would change `fdroid/app.solyto.yml` must be flagged.\n79\t\n80\tTASK-7: Write the final report `docs/reports/android-homescreen-widgets.md`. It should contain: a summary/verdict (feasible yes/no and recommended approach), current state (TASK-1), options comparison (TASK-2), data/auth (TASK-3), candidate widgets (TASK-4), deep linking (TASK-5), constraints and risks (TASK-6), a phased rough plan (for example an MVP shortcut widget, then a data widget), open questions for the maintainer, and dated sources.\n81\t  files: docs/reports/android-homescreen-widgets.md (new)\n82\t  depends: TASK-2, TASK-3, TASK-4, TASK-5, TASK-6\n83\t  risk: low. It is documentation only, with no build or runtime impact.\n84\t\n85\t## Unclear / for the maintainer\n86\t\n87\t- The brief has no \"Why\" and no \"Out of scope\". The analyst assumes the\n88\t  report stays technology-agnostic about *which* widgets ship. It proposes\n89\t  candidates and does not pick a final set.\n90\t- It is unclear whether introducing Kotlin (needed for Glance) is acceptable\n91\t  in this repo. The report should present this as a trade-off, not assume it.\n92\t- It is unclear whether moving the auth token out of the WebView into native\n93\t  storage is acceptable from a security/privacy standpoint. The report\n94\t  should surface this as a decision for the maintainer.\n95\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"debe97b1-540a-4869-be59-f7c5d9cf8b55","uuid":"481da24b-fcb6-47d5-9507-ace5520fb71c","timestamp":"2026-10-17T14:51:59.378Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/docs/jobs/archive/opinion_audit-android-homescreen-widgets/tasks.md","content":"# Tasks: Audit: android homescreen widgets\n\nid: opinion\nanalyst: @analyst\ndate: 2026-10-15\n\n## Scope\n\nThis is a **report-only** job. The deliverable is one markdown file,\n`docs/reports/android-homescreen-widgets.md`. It answers two questions: can\nsolyto ship Android home-screen widgets, and how would we build them?\n**No code, Gradle, manifest, `patch/`, `frontend/` or `fdroid/` changes.** If\na prototype ever seems necessary to answer a question, stop and record it in\nthe report as an open question. Do not build one.\n\n`docs/reports/` does not exist yet. Creating it (by writing the report file)\nis expected.\n\n## Context gathered by the analyst (for the developer)\n\n- The native side is small. `android/app/src/main/java/app/solyto/` holds\n  `MainActivity` (extends `BridgeActivity`), `PullToRefreshLayout`, and one\n  custom plugin, `PullToRefreshPlugin`, registered with\n  `registerPlugin(...)`. That plugin is the existing pattern for a custom\n  JS↔native bridge.\n- `AndroidManifest.xml` has one activity (`singleTask`). Its intent filters\n  are MAIN/LAUNCHER, SEND text/plain (→ `https://my.solyto.app/share?q=`),\n  and VIEW on `@string/custom_url_scheme`. It has no receivers or services,\n  and its only permission is INTERNET.\n- Auth: the bearer token, its expiry, and the user live in **WebView\n  localStorage** under the key `auth` (`frontend/src/lib/state/Auth.svelte.ts`,\n  `LS_AUTH_KEY`). The token expires (`expires_at`) and is renewed through\n  `apiRoutes.auth.refresh`. Native code cannot read WebView localStorage\n  directly. This is the main architectural problem for any widget that shows\n  live data.\n- The API base URL is also in localStorage (`custom_api_url`, in\n  `frontend/src/lib/config/platform.ts`). Its default is `https://api.solyto.app`.\n- `frontend/` is vendored. Any JS-side bridge call (for example \"push widget\n  data to native\") would have to land in the canonical `../app` repo, gated\n  on `IS_MOBILE`. The report must say this explicitly.\n- Distribution: GitHub Releases plus F-Droid (`fdroid/app.solyto.yml`,\n  `scripts/build-fdroid.sh`, offline, Node 20). New native dependencies must\n  be FOSS and available from Maven Central or Google's Maven repository, with\n  no proprietary or GMS dependencies.\n- SDK levels: minSdk 24, target and compile 36, Java only (no Kotlin set up\n  yet; check `android/build.gradle` and `android/app/build.gradle` to confirm).\n\n## Task breakdown\n\nTASK-1: Inventory the current Android/Capacitor setup that is relevant to widgets: Java vs. Kotlin, AGP/Gradle versions, AndroidX deps, Capacitor plugins in use, minSdk/targetSdk, and existing intent handling. Summarize it as a \"current state\" section.\n  files (read-only): android/build.gradle, android/app/build.gradle, android/variables.gradle, android/app/capacitor.build.gradle, android/capacitor.settings.gradle, android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml, android/app/src/main/java/app/solyto/*.java, capacitor.config.json, package.json\n  depends: none\n  risk: low. It is a read-only inventory.\n\nTASK-2: Research and compare the technical options for widgets in a Capacitor app: (a) classic `AppWidgetProvider` + `RemoteViews` (Java, no new deps); (b) Jetpack Glance (needs Kotlin + Compose compiler); (c) existing community Capacitor widget plugins, checked for maintenance status, Capacitor 7 compatibility, licence, and F-Droid suitability. Cover API-level constraints such as collection widgets/`RemoteCollectionItems` and API 31+ widget features versus minSdk 24.\n  files: docs/reports/android-homescreen-widgets.md (new)\n  depends: TASK-1\n  risk: medium. External library status changes over time, so cite sources with dates and do not assert anything from memory.\n\nTASK-3: Analyse the data/auth problem: how a widget gets data and credentials when the token lives in WebView localStorage. Compare at least: (1) the web app pushes a pre-rendered widget snapshot to native via a custom Capacitor plugin (SharedPreferences), so there are no native network calls; (2) the web app hands the token and API URL to native storage, and the widget fetches through WorkManager or `updatePeriodMillis`, with token-expiry/refresh handling; (3) static \"shortcut\" widgets with no data at all. Note the security implications of copying the token out of the WebView (for example EncryptedSharedPreferences, now deprecated, versus Keystore).\n  files: docs/reports/android-homescreen-widgets.md\n  depends: TASK-1\n  risk: medium. This is the decisive section, and it touches auth design. Present it as options, not a decision.\n\nTASK-4: Map candidate widgets to existing features and API endpoints (for example today's todos, next calendar events, quick-add/check-in shortcut, pinned note), using `frontend/src/lib/config/apiRoutes.ts` and the state classes. For each candidate, give the data it needs, whether it needs interaction (tap-to-complete versus open-in-app deep link), and a rough complexity rating.\n  files (read-only): frontend/src/lib/config/apiRoutes.ts, frontend/src/lib/state/Todos.svelte.ts, frontend/src/lib/state/Calendars.svelte.ts, frontend/src/lib/state/CheckInData.svelte.ts; output in docs/reports/android-homescreen-widgets.md\n  depends: TASK-3\n  risk: low. It is descriptive, but the final widget selection belongs to the maintainer, so it should be presented as candidates.\n\nTASK-5: Describe how widget taps would open the app at a specific route (PendingIntent → `MainActivity` → `loadUrl(\"https://my.solyto.app/<route>\")`, the same approach as `handleShareIntent`), including the `singleTask`/`onNewIntent` behaviour.\n  files (read-only): android/app/src/main/java/app/solyto/MainActivity.java, android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml; output in report\n  depends: TASK-1\n  risk: low. The pattern already exists in the code.\n\nTASK-6: Assess cross-cutting impact and constraints: vendored-frontend workflow (JS changes go to `../app`, gated on `IS_MOBILE`), F-Droid build implications (new Gradle deps, Kotlin plugin, reproducibility, `fdroid/app.solyto.yml` flagged), i18n (widget strings in Android `res/values-*` for en/de/fr/es versus the web i18n tables), light/dark theming with the existing `colors.xml`, battery and update-frequency limits, and testing approach.\n  files (read-only): scripts/build-fdroid.sh, scripts/vendor.sh, fdroid/app.solyto.yml, .github/workflows/release.yml, android/app/src/main/res/values/colors.xml; output in report\n  depends: TASK-2, TASK-3\n  risk: low. It is analysis only, but any recommendation that would change `fdroid/app.solyto.yml` must be flagged.\n\nTASK-7: Write the final report `docs/reports/android-homescreen-widgets.md`. It should contain: a summary/verdict (feasible yes/no and recommended approach), current state (TASK-1), options comparison (TASK-2), data/auth (TASK-3), candidate widgets (TASK-4), deep linking (TASK-5), constraints and risks (TASK-6), a phased rough plan (for example an MVP shortcut widget, then a data widget), open questions for the maintainer, and dated sources.\n  files: docs/reports/android-homescreen-widgets.md (new)\n  depends: TASK-2, TASK-3, TASK-4, TASK-5, TASK-6\n  risk: low. It is documentation only, with no build or runtime impact.\n\n## Unclear / for the maintainer\n\n- The brief has no \"Why\" and no \"Out of scope\". The analyst assumes the\n  report stays technology-agnostic about *which* widgets ship. It proposes\n  candidates and does not pick a final set.\n- It is unclear whether introducing Kotlin (needed for Glance) is acceptable\n  in this repo. The report should present this as a trade-off, not assume it.\n- It is unclear whether moving the auth token out of the WebView into native\n  storage is acceptable from a security/privacy standpoint. The report\n  should surface this as a decision for the maintainer.\n","numLines":95,"startLine":1,"totalLines":95}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01YFppTDFvxQDanbPmdm7SgC","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
