{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KBBpGQ3fvjAGCoAC2D4DZB","type":"tool_result","content":"2:Verify your developer identity information - Play Console Help\n34:Android developer verification\n54:Verify your developer identity information\n59:Verify your developer identity information\n60:As part of our ongoing efforts to keep Google Play a safe and trusted platform, you must complete the verification described on this page when setting up your Google Play Console developer account. This helps us to better understand developers and help prevent bad actors from distributing malware. Developers can complete verification in Play Console 60 days before their individual deadline by following the steps in \n68:This section describes the information you need to provide that will be shown to users on Google Play after it's been verified.\n70:As part of creating your Google Play developer account, you will be asked to link a Google Payments profile. This profile helps Google Play verify your developer identity.\n73:. The type of payment profile is based on the type of developer account you want to create (personal or organization). Payments profiles for personal accounts and organization accounts have different information verification requirements.\n74:Personal account\n75:If you create a developer account for personal use, and a Google Payments profile has not been verified, you will be required to provide the following information in Play Console as part of verifying your identity details:\n76:Official government identity document\n77:Organization account\n78:If you create a developer account for an organization, and a Google Payments profile has not been verified, you will be required to provide the following information in Play Console as part of verifying your identity details:\n79:D-U-N-S number (unless your developer account is for a known government organization or agency)\n80:Official government identity document\n81:Official organization document\n83:: Applying for a D-U-N-S number through Dun & Bradstreet is a completely free process. Be aware of third-party services that may attempt to charge high administrative fees for this free registration.\n84:Government organizations or agencies\n85:If you create a developer account for a known government organization or agency, and a Google Payments profile has not been verified, you won't need to provide a D-U-N-S number.\n86:If your developer account is for a government organization or agency and you are prompted to provide a D-U-N-S number when trying to create a payment profile as part of the verification process, you can \n90: if you don’t have access to Play Console. They can enable your account to complete verification without providing a D-U-N-S number.\n94: provide a D-U-N-S number.\n101:All developers must provide and verify a developer email address that will be shown on Google Play. This is to help Google Play users learn more about the developer.\n102:The following steps describe how to verify your developer email address when \n103:providing contact information\n===\n14:Submit feedback\n15:Send feedback on...\n40:Privacy, Deception and Device Abuse\n41:Use of SDKs In Apps\n47:Spam, Functionality, and User Experience\n62:You must be at least 18 years of age to sign up for a Play Console account.\n63:Using your Google Account, \n66:Once you have an account, you can use Play Console to \n73:Step 3: Pay registration fee\n74:There is a US$25 one-time registration fee that you can pay with the following credit or debit cards:\n87: Developers with personal developer accounts must meet certain app testing requirements before they can distribute their app on Google Play. For more information, go to \n91:As part of our ongoing efforts to keep Google Play a safe and trusted platform, you must complete the verifications described on this page when setting up your Play Console developer account. This helps us to better understand developers and help prevent bad actors from distributing malware. Verification requirements vary based on the account type you selected in Step 4.\n96: To process your request for a Play Developer account you may be asked for a valid government ID and a credit card, both under your legal name. If this information is determined to be invalid, your registration fee will not be refunded.\n98:Developers with personal accounts created after November 13, 2023, must meet specific testing requirements before they can make their app available on Google Play. Read \n101:Starting in early 2024, developers with new personal accounts will be required to verify that they have access to an Android device using the Play Console mobile app before they can make their app available on Google Play. Read\nShell cwd was reset to /workspace","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8263a2d0-7a74-4d55-80cb-40597ee31eb7","uuid":"2e39f0c7-7297-439d-bd72-4c69e176d5d1","timestamp":"2026-10-17T14:54:33.644Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"2:Verify your developer identity information - Play Console Help\n34:Android developer verification\n54:Verify your developer identity information\n59:Verify your developer identity information\n60:As part of our ongoing efforts to keep Google Play a safe and trusted platform, you must complete the verification described on this page when setting up your Google Play Console developer account. This helps us to better understand developers and help prevent bad actors from distributing malware. Developers can complete verification in Play Console 60 days before their individual deadline by following the steps in \n68:This section describes the information you need to provide that will be shown to users on Google Play after it's been verified.\n70:As part of creating your Google Play developer account, you will be asked to link a Google Payments profile. This profile helps Google Play verify your developer identity.\n73:. The type of payment profile is based o