{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019GzDy6qLDqdfCtMQ8ht2WL","type":"tool_result","content":"Apps that enable users to create, generate, upload, share, or otherwise contribute content present particular challenges, including intellectual property infringement, nonconsensual intimate imagery, and anonymous bullying. This includes social media, user-generated content platforms, and AI content creation apps. To prevent abuse, such apps must include:\nA method for filtering objectionable material from being posted to the app\nA mechanism to report offensive content and timely responses to concerns\nThe ability to block abusive users from the service\nPublished contact information so users can easily reach you\n2:5.1.1(v) - Offering account deletion in your app - Guideline reference - App Review - Help - Apple Developer\n30:Web\n145:5.1.1(v) - Offering account deletion in your app\n154:5.1.1(v) - Offering account deletion in your app\n177:5.1.1(v) - Offering account deletion in your app\n181:5.1.1(v) - Offering account deletion in your app\n182:Apps submitted to the App Store that support account creation must also let people initiate deletion of their account within the app.\n184:Account deletion guidance\n185:Deleting an account is a major decision, so the process to initiate and confirm it must be straightforward and transparent:\n186:Make the account deletion option easy to find in your app.\n189: You may include additional options, but only offering to temporarily deactivate or disable an account is insufficient.\n190:If people need to visit a website to finish deleting their account, include a link directly to the page on your website where they can complete the process.\n191: On tvOS, you can present a static URL or QR code that links directly to the account deletion page.\n197:Follow applicable legal requirements for storing and retaining user account information and for handling account deletion. This includes complying with local laws where your app is available. If you have questions regarding your legal obligations, check with your legal counsel.\n199:Can I direct people to a customer service flow to complete account deletion?\napplefee.txt:130:D-U-N-S\napplefee.txt:132:Fee waivers\napplefee.txt:252:Account and organizational data sharing\napplefee.txt:256:Configure the Apps and Books for Organizations API\napplefee.txt:322:D-U-N-S\napplefee.txt:323:Fee waivers\napplefee.txt:390:Account and organizational data sharing\napplefee.txt:392:Configure the Apps and Books for Organizations API\napplefee.txt:442: your apps for beta testing and distribution, view analytics, and more. You can enroll as an individual or enroll your organization.\napplefee.txt:443:Enroll your organization in the Apple Developer Enterprise Program\napplefee.txt:444:Organizations that require private distribution directly to employees through a secure internal system or a Mobile Device \napplefee.txt:448:Organizations enrolled in a developer program can invite additional registered Apple developers to join their team and give them access to development and distribution resources for the team. The invitation is sent by email, and if the recipient isn’t registered, they’ll be asked to register during the acceptance process.\nenroll.txt:118:To become an Apple Developer Program member, you can enroll as an individual or enroll your organization. Learn about what you’ll need and the requirements to enroll.\nenroll.txt:120: Enrolling as an individual\nenroll.txt:121:If you’re an individual or sole proprietor/single person business, you’ll need an Apple Account with \nenroll.txt:129: Enrolling your organization\nenroll.txt:130:If you’re enrolling your organization, you’ll need an Apple Account with \nenroll.txt:132: turned on. We recommend that your Apple Account uses your email address from your organization. Make sure both your first and last names are your legal names. Using an alias, nickname, or company name as your first or last name will cause a delay in the approval of your enrollment.\nenroll.txt:135: As the person enrolling your organization in the Apple Developer Program, you’ll be the Account Holder and must have the legal authority to bind your organization to legal agreements. You must be the organization’s owner/founder, executive team member, senior project lead, or an employee with legal authority granted to you by a senior employee.\nenroll.txt:137: Your organization must be a legal entity that can enter into contracts with Apple. We do not accept DBAs, fictitious business names, trade names, or branches. Your organization’s name will be displayed as the seller name of your apps on the App Store.\nShell cwd was reset to /workspace","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8263a2d0-7a74-4d55-80cb-40597ee31eb7","uuid":"81324177-f5be-4953-bcac-ae67636d73d5","timestamp":"2026-10-17T14:54:59.707Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"Apps that enable users to create, generate, upload, share, or otherwise contribute content present particular challenges, including intellectual property infringement, nonconsensual intimate imagery, and anonymous bullying. This includes social media, user-generated content platforms, and AI content creation apps. To prevent abuse, such apps must include:\nA method for filtering objectionable material from being posted to the app\nA mechanism to report offensive content and timely responses to concerns\nThe ability to block abusive users from the service\nPublished contact information so users can easily reach you\n2:5.1.1(v) - Offering account deletion in your app - Guideline reference - App Review - Help - Apple Developer\n30:Web\n145:5.1.1(v) - Offering account deletion in your app\n154:5.1.1(v) - Offering account deletion in your app\n177:5.1.1(v) - Offering account deletion in your app\n181:5.1.1(v) - Offering account deletion in your app\n182:Apps submitted to the App Store that support account creation must also let people initiate deletion of their account within the app.\n184:Account deletion guidance\n185:Deleting an account is a major decision, so the process to initiate and confirm it must be straightforward and transparent:\n186:Make the account deletion option easy to find in your app.\n189: You may include additional options, but only offering to temporarily deactivate or disable an account is insufficient.\n190:If people need to visit a website to finish deleting their account, include a link directly to the page on your website where they can complete the process.\n191: On tvOS, you can present a static URL or QR code that links directly to the account deletion page.\n197:Follow applicable legal requirements for storing and retaining user account information and for handling account deletion. This includes complying with local laws where your app is available. If you have questions regarding your legal obligations, check with your legal counsel.\n199:Can I direct people to a customer service flow to complete account deletion?\napplefee.txt:130:D-U-N-S\napplefee.txt:132:Fee waivers\napplefee.txt:252:Account and organizational data sharing\napplefee.txt:256:Configure the Apps and Books for Organizations API\napplefee.txt:322:D-U-N-S\napplefee.txt:323:Fee waivers\napplefee.txt:390:Account and organizational data sharing\napplefee.txt:392:Configure the Apps and Books for Organizations API\napplefee.txt:442: your apps for beta testing and distribution, view analytics, and more. You can enroll as an individual or enroll your organization.\napplefee.txt:443:Enroll your organization in the Apple Developer Enterprise Program\napplefee.txt:444:Organizations that require private distribution directly to employees through a secure internal system or a Mobile Device \napplefee.txt:448:Organizations enrolled in a developer program can invite additional registered Apple developers to join their team and give them access to development and distribution resources for the team. The invitation is sent by email, and if the recipient isn’t registered, they’ll be asked to register during the acceptance process.\nenroll.txt:118:To become an Apple Developer Program member, you can enroll as an individual or enroll your organization. Learn about what you’ll need and the requirements to enroll.\nenroll.txt:120: Enrolling as an individual\nenroll.txt:121:If you’re an individual or sole proprietor/single person business, you’ll need an Apple Account with \nenroll.txt:129: Enrolling your organization\nenroll.txt:130:If you’re enrolling your organization, you’ll need an Apple Account with \nenroll.txt:132: turned on. We recommend that your Apple Account uses your email address from your organization. Make sure both your first and last names are your legal names. Using an alias, nickname, or company name as your first or last name will cause a delay in the approval of your enrollment.\nenroll.txt:135: As the person enrolling your organization in the Apple Developer Program, you’ll be the Account Holder and must have the legal authority to bind your organization to legal agreements. You must be the organization’s owner/founder, executive team member, senior project lead, or an employee with legal authority granted to you by a senior employee.\nenroll.txt:137: Your organization must be a legal entity that can enter into contracts with Apple. We do not accept DBAs, fictitious business names, trade names, or branches. Your organization’s name will be displayed as the seller name of your apps on the App Store.","stderr":"\nShell cwd was reset to /workspace","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_019GzDy6qLDqdfCtMQ8ht2WL","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
