{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PyVCTBt4AgxTxZx9DBMrdz","type":"tool_result","content":"254 capenv7.txt\n213 capios7.txt\n312 capspm.txt\n142:homebrew\n154:macOS\n159:Xcode\n160:Xcode Command Line Tools\n161:Homebrew\n162:Cocoapods\n163:Once you've installed the core requirements, as well as Xcode, Xcode Command Line Tools, and Cocoapods, you'll be able to create both iOS applications and PWAs.\n164:Xcode\n166:Xcode is Apple's IDE for creating native macOS, iOS, and iPadOS applications. You can install Xcode by \n168: on your Mac. Capacitor 7 requires a minimum of Xcode 16.0.\n169:Xcode Command Line Tools\n171:The Xcode command line tools are additional tools not included with the core of Xcode that are required for building and testing your application. Once Xcode has been installed, you can install the Xcode Command Line Tools by running the following command in your terminal:\n172:xcode-select \n175:xcode-select \n177:# /Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer\ncapspm.txt:2:Swift Package Manager | Capacitor Documentation\ncapspm.txt:84:Swift Package Manager\ncapspm.txt:133:Swift Package Manager\ncapspm.txt:134:Swift Packages are Apple's new first-party tool for software dependencies. Traditionally Capacitor has used CocoaPods for managing dependencies internally and for plugins, however now is the time to move to a supported solution.\ncapspm.txt:135:Since Capacitor 6, you can choose between using CocoaPods or Swift Package Manager (SPM). Almost all current capacitor-team supported plugins support SPM, namely the plugins in \ncapspm.txt:138:We've tried our best to make sure you don't have to change much about how you work with Capacitor to use SPM, but there are a few things to understand.\ncapspm.txt:141:When a Capacitor project is using SPM we use a 'Base SPM' package that will serve as the place that references all of your projects dependencies:\ncapspm.txt:142:The Capacitor CLI will modify the CapApp-SPM package when you sync new plugins. It is important you do not touch the contents here because the CLI can and will change things.\ncapspm.txt:143:Using SPM in a new Capacitor project\ncapspm.txt:153:--packagemanager SPM\ncapspm.txt:155:npx cap add ios --packagemanager SPM\ncapspm.txt:159:Add and use a Capacitor Plugin with SPM\ncapspm.txt:164:Then let's sync the web app. This will add the App plugin SPM to the iOS project:\ncapspm.txt:167:Using SPM in an existing Capacitor Project\ncapspm.txt:177:npx cap add ios --packagemanager SPM\ncapspm.txt:178:. This will remove the CocoaPods template project and replace it with the SPM template project.\ncapspm.txt:181:The Capacitor CLI has a command to help migrate from CocoaPods to Swift Package Manager. However, two manual steps are still required. Some things to note are: Projects with Cordova plugins should work, but some plugins may not work correctly as we have to generate a \ncapspm.txt:183: file for them. In addition, projects that use capacitor plugins that do not have SPM versions available, will fail to work properly and will show a warning about the incompatible plugins during migration and when running \ncapspm.txt:187:npx cap spm-migration-assistant\ncapspm.txt:192: removing CocoaPods\nShell cwd was reset to /workspace","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8263a2d0-7a74-4d55-80cb-40597ee31eb7","uuid":"61c598a8-9e8d-4a53-ae5a-d6784682a89c","timestamp":"2026-10-17T14:55:10.195Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"254 capenv7.txt\n213 capios7.txt\n312 capspm.txt\n142:homebrew\n154:macOS\n159:Xcode\n160:Xcode Command Line Tools\n161:Homebrew\n162:Cocoapods\n163:Once you've installed the core requirements, as well as Xcode, Xcode Command Line Tools, and Cocoapods, you'll be able to create both iOS applications and PWAs.\n164:Xcode\n166:Xcode is Apple's IDE for creating native macOS, iOS, and iPadOS applications. You can install Xcode by \n168: on your Mac. Capacitor 7 requires a minimum of Xcode 16.0.\n169:Xcode Command Line Tools\n171:The Xcode command line tools are additional tools not included with the core of Xcode that are required for building and testing your application. Once Xcode has been installed, you can install the Xcode Command Line Tools by running the following command in your terminal:\n172:xcode-select \n175:xcode-select \n177:# /Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer\ncapspm.txt:2:Swift Package Manager | Capacitor Documentation\ncapspm.txt:84:Swift Package Manager\ncapspm.txt:133:Swift Package Manager\ncapspm.txt:134:Swift Packages are Apple's new first-party tool for software dependencies. Traditionally Capacitor has used CocoaPods for managing dependencies internally and for plugins, however now is the time to move to a supported solution.\ncapspm.txt:135:Since Capacitor 6, you can choose between using CocoaPods or Swift Package Manager (SPM). Almost all current capacitor-team supported plugins support SPM, namely the plugins in \ncapspm.txt:138:We've tried our best to make sure you don't have to change much about how you work with Capacitor to use SPM, but there are a few things to understand.\ncapspm.txt:141:When a Capacitor project is using SPM we use a 'Base SPM' package that will serve as the place that references all of your projects dependencies:\ncapspm.txt:142:The Capacitor CLI will modify the CapApp-SPM package when you sync new plugins. It is important you do not touch the contents here because the CLI can and will change things.\ncapspm.txt:143:Using SPM in a new Capacitor project\ncapspm.txt:153:--packagemanager SPM\ncapspm.txt:155:npx cap add ios --packagemanager SPM\ncapspm.txt:159:Add and use a Capacitor Plugin with SPM\ncapspm.txt:164:Then let's sync the web app. This will add the App plugin SPM to the iOS project:\ncapspm.txt:167:Using SPM in an existing Capacitor Project\ncapspm.txt:177:npx cap add ios --packagemanager SPM\ncapspm.txt:178:. This will remove the CocoaPods template project and replace it with the SPM template project.\ncapspm.txt:181:The Capacitor CLI has a command to help migrate from CocoaPods to Swift Package Manager. However, two manual steps are still required. Some things to note are: Projects with Cordova plugins should work, but some plugins may not work correctly as we have to generate a \ncapspm.txt:183: file for them. In addition, projects that use capacitor plugins that do not have SPM versions available, will fail to work properly and will show a warning about the incompatible plugins during migration and when running \ncapspm.txt:187:npx cap spm-migration-assistant\ncapspm.txt:192: removing CocoaPods","stderr":"\nShell cwd was reset to /workspace","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01PyVCTBt4AgxTxZx9DBMrdz","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
