{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NcoLptz4s2Y77p52TCDBd2","type":"tool_result","content":"498: From August 2021, new apps are required to publish with the\n501: Google Play. New apps larger than 200 MB are now supported by either\n507:TV apps are required to\n560:the compressed APKs required to install your app (for example, the base APK +\n604:the Google Play Store and are missing one or more required split APKs—fail on\n63:for your Android App Bundles (\n64:.aab\n65:). By letting Google sign the optimized distribution APKs generated from your app bundles, you gain access to valuable services like \n93:: You use this key to sign your app bundle before uploading it to the Play Console. Google uses it to verify your identity. If compromised or lost, Google can reset this key for you.\n128:For new apps\n132:: Generate a keystore to sign your release app bundle. You can \n137:Upload your app bundle: \n138:Go to your Play Console and prepare a new release. By default, when you upload your app bundle, your app is automatically enrolled in quantum-ready, hybrid signing with Google-generated keys.\nFor new apps\nCreate a new app\n: Your app will be automatically enrolled in quantum-ready, hybrid signing with Google-generated keys. We also generate a separate classical key to be used in signature blocks that are recognized on devices Android 16-.\nCreate an upload key\n: Generate a keystore to sign your release app bundle. You can \ngenerate it in Android Studio\n or use the \nJava keytool utility from the command line\n.\nUpload your app bundle: \nGo to your Play Console and prepare a new release. By default, when you upload your app bundle, your app is automatically enrolled in quantum-ready, hybrid signing with Google-generated keys.\nChange your app signing key (optional)\n: Advanced developers who want to manage their own key can change this default before there is a release rolled out in open testing track or production track. You can do this by navigating to \nFor existing apps\nIf you currently manage your own keys and upload APKs, you can upgrade to Play App Signing to take advantage of app bundles and Play enhancements.\nGo to \nProtected with Play > Play Store distribution > Go to Play app signing\n in the Play Console.\nAccept the terms of service, if you haven't already.\nTransfer a copy of your original key:\n Download the PEPK tool and follow the unified step-by-step instructions to encrypt and upload your existing app signing key from any repository.\nCreate a new upload key (recommended):\n Generate a new key in Android Studio to use as your upload key going forward, and register its certificate in the Play Console.\nRegister with API providers\nIf your app uses APIs (like Google Maps, OAuth, or Facebook Login), those services authenticate your app using your \napp signing key's fingerprint\n.\nBecause Google signs the final APK, \nyou must register the Google-held app signing key fingerprint with your API providers\n, not just your local upload key.\nGo to\nShell cwd was reset to /workspace","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8263a2d0-7a74-4d55-80cb-40597ee31eb7","uuid":"5ace59a9-85ab-4e4b-afa1-decddf9a572a","timestamp":"2026-10-17T14:57:34.563Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"498: From August 2021, new apps are required to publish with the\n501: Google Play. New apps larger than 200 MB are now supported by either\n507:TV apps are required to\n560:the compressed APKs required to install your app (for example, the base APK +\n604:the Google Play Store and are missing one or more required split APKs—fail on\n63:for your Android App Bundles (\n64:.aab\n65:). By letting Google sign the optimized distribution APKs generated from your app bundles, you gain access to valuable services like \n93:: You use this key to sign your app bundle before uploading it to the Play Console. Google uses it to verify your identity. If compromised or lost, Google can reset this