{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg81bcNp9V7oJXXyywpJ3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written the report at `docs/reports/play-store-and-app-store.md`. It lists every step for Google Play and the App Store. Neither store can accept the app yet, and the three things that block it are fixes in the backend and `../app`, not in this repo. Policy facts are taken from the official Google, Apple, Capacitor, F-Droid and FSF pages as they read today (2026-10-17), and the report lists them with that date. I made no code, Gradle, manifest, `fdroid/`, `fastlane/`, CI or signing changes, created no iOS project, and ran no release or store tooling. Everything is committed on `report/jail_play-store-and-app-store-strategy`, with a summary in `implementation.md`.\n\n**Blockers for both stores:**\n- **No account deletion.** I checked: there is no delete endpoint in `apiRoutes.ts` and no deletion option in the UI. Google requires both an in-app path and a web page where users can request deletion. Apple requires that users can start deletion inside the app.\n- **Shared user content has no moderation.** Feature requests with comments, public profiles and friend feeds let users see each other's content, but there is no report or block option and no terms of use to accept. Both stores require these, or the features have to be hidden in the store builds.\n- **The privacy policy isn't reachable in the app.** It's only a section on the landing page (`solyto.app/#privacy`). The in-app legal links only appear if `PUBLIC_PRIVACY_URL` is set, and `build-fdroid.sh` doesn't set it, so they are most likely hidden in the app.\n\n**Google Play** needs little technical work:\n- The app already targets API 36, which meets the current deadline.\n- `./gradlew bundleRelease` produces the required App Bundle (AAB) with no Gradle change.\n- Most of the work is the account and the forms: a US$25 fee, identity verification, and for a new personal account a closed test with 12 testers for 14 days.\n- The current screenshots fail Play's rules (518×1149, transparent background, too tall), and the required 1024×500 feature graphic doesn't exist.\n- **Signing is your call:** uploading the existing key to Google lets GitHub APK users switch to Play without reinstalling. F-Droid signs with its own key, so F-Droid users would need to reinstall either way.\n\n**Apple App Store** is a lot more work and has a real chance of rejection:\n- There's no iOS project yet. You'd need a Mac with Xcode 26 or later, the Apple membership at 99 USD a year, and iOS versions of the share target, deep links, pull-to-refresh and screen-edge handling.\n- On iOS the app runs at `capacitor://my.solyto.app` instead of `https://`. That needs a CORS change on the API and may break passkeys; only a test build will tell.\n- Web push notifications won't work inside the iOS app.\n- The biggest review risk is Apple's rule against apps that are just a \"repackaged website\". Adding iOS-native features lowers it.\n- Sign in with Apple isn't required, because solyto only uses its own accounts.\n\n**Needs action whatever you decide about the stores:** Google's new developer verification for sideloaded apps starts on 30 Sep 2026 in four countries and goes worldwide in 2027. Registering `app.solyto` together with F-Droid's signing key keeps F-Droid installs working smoothly.\n\nThe report also covers the licence question: there are no GPL/AGPL runtime dependencies. If anyone besides you contributed code to `../app`, that matters for the App Store, but I couldn't check that from here. It also includes a phased plan (Play can launch before iOS), costs, the work split by repo, and 12 open questions. The main ones are personal vs organization account, the Play signing choice, what to do with the user-content features and push, and whether a Mac is available.\n\nSeveral things are marked as unverified in the report, most importantly:\n- whether Capacitor 7 builds with Xcode 26 (if not, you'd need to upgrade to Capacitor 8, which affects Android too)\n- whether passkeys work in the iOS app\n- whether Android's WebView supports push\n\nTwo smaller things came up outside the scope:\n- The `versionCode` formula collides once the minor or patch number goes above 99; for example, 1.0.100 gets the same code as 1.1.0.\n- `allowBackup=\"true\"` may copy the login token into Google's device backup."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2419,"cache_read_input_tokens":139770,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2419},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8263a2d0-7a74-4d55-80cb-40597ee31eb7","uuid":"8e22d077-053e-421d-8a30-2b6286f76a11","timestamp":"2026-10-17T15:02:06.766Z","request_id":"req_011Cg81bbzVrzSyAbdQeT198"}
